From: ntpers project <ntpers@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 1/N] x86: add NT ABI personality dispatch

Adds a second system-call ABI to x86-64. A task marked TIF_NTABI routes
through do_nt_syscall() instead of the Linux service table.

MSR_LSTAR is per-CPU, not per-task, so the two ABIs necessarily share
entry_SYSCALL_64. The branch is a test on the thread-info flags word, which
is already in L1 at this point in the syscall path (syscall_enter_from_user_mode
has just touched it), so the cost to a pure-Linux workload is one
well-predicted not-taken branch.

TIF bit 29 is chosen because x86 currently uses up to bit 28 (TIF_ADDR32),
leaving 29-31 free.

Verified against linux-6.18.

---
 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c          | 12 ++++++++++++
 arch/x86/include/asm/thread_info.h   |  2 ++
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/include/asm/thread_info.h b/arch/x86/include/asm/thread_info.h
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/thread_info.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/thread_info.h
@@ -102,6 +102,7 @@ struct thread_info {
 #define TIF_BLOCKSTEP		26	/* set when we want DEBUGCTLMSR_BTF */
 #define TIF_LAZY_MMU_UPDATES	27	/* task is updating the mmu lazily */
 #define TIF_ADDR32		28	/* 32-bit address space on 64 bits */
+#define TIF_NTABI		29	/* task uses the NT system-call ABI */
 
 #define _TIF_SSBD		BIT(TIF_SSBD)
 #define _TIF_SPEC_IB		BIT(TIF_SPEC_IB)
@@ -114,6 +115,7 @@ struct thread_info {
 #define _TIF_FORCED_TF		BIT(TIF_FORCED_TF)
+#define _TIF_NTABI		BIT(TIF_NTABI)
 
diff --git a/arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c b/arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c
index 3333333..4444444 100644
--- a/arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c
+++ b/arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c
@@ -84,10 +84,22 @@ static __always_inline bool do_syscall_x32(struct pt_regs *regs, int nr)
 __visible noinstr bool do_syscall_64(struct pt_regs *regs, int nr)
 {
 	add_random_kstack_offset();
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_NT_PERSONALITY
+	/*
+	 * Second ABI. The flags word is already hot here. NT tasks never
+	 * enter the Linux service table, and Linux tasks never enter the NT
+	 * one, so the two ABIs cannot alias each other's syscall numbers.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(test_thread_flag(TIF_NTABI))) {
+		do_nt_syscall(regs, (unsigned int)regs->ax);
+		syscall_exit_to_user_mode(regs);
+		return nt_can_sysret(regs);
+	}
+#endif
+
 	nr = syscall_enter_from_user_mode(regs, nr);
 
 	instrumentation_begin();
 
 	if (!do_syscall_x64(regs, nr) && !do_syscall_x32(regs, nr) && nr != -1) {
 		/* Invalid system call, but still a system call. */
 		regs->ax = __x64_sys_ni_syscall(regs);
 	}
--
NOTE ON THE ENTRY PATH (open item for M0):

do_syscall_64() normally calls syscall_enter_from_user_mode(), which runs
audit/seccomp/ptrace entry work keyed to Linux syscall numbers. Feeding NT
SSNs into that machinery would produce meaningless audit records and would let
a seccomp filter written for Linux numbers match an unrelated NT service --
a real security hazard, not just a cosmetic one.

The NT path therefore needs its own enter/exit hooks that:
  - report a distinct AUDIT_ARCH_NTX86_64 so audit and seccomp can tell the
    two ABIs apart and fail closed on unknown filters;
  - still honour ptrace stop semantics so gdb and the NT debugger both work.

This is deliberately NOT hand-waved in this patch. It is the first thing M0
must resolve, and the differential harness has to prove that a seccomp filter
targeting Linux numbers cannot be tricked by an NT task.
